{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793301905,"nonce":"Q9SiGZZbwc0lsqD6F6751oUtoF0=","title":"Reuse LLVM compilation artifacts for interactive WASM rebuilds","message":"Related AIR-cache work: c78c3b39b7af7f1f0c20b18a6640d766a6f4522a487790bd8d11663b3b2cd050\nRayDE preview latency: 076442e62758032b49784eb4b7634d7ed711bd5645cac41025011cdbb1c576bb\n\nA Pocket Studio app with published Ashui 0.2.2, full Haxe DCE, AIR O3, LLVM O3 and codegen level 0 still spends most warm-rebuild time recreating LLVM IR and objects. An isolated warm sample reused all 2,699 optimized AIR bodies with zero misses/writes, but Ash still took 10.506 s: LLVM IR 3.589 s, backend 5.759 s. LLVM O1 only changed the warm sample to 10.300 s. These are local diagnostic samples, not cross-platform throughput claims. The complete browser preview from fresh AIR now takes about 25 s after RayDE DCE and release-session fixes; unchanged bundles reuse RayDE cache in about 1.5 s. Code edits still need compilation.\n\nInvestigate bounded persistent reuse of LLVM/shard/object artifacts across builds, especially a single edited function. Cache identity must cover target/CPU/features, compiler and LLVM versions, optimization and fiber/exception settings, native bindings, all type/global/function-table dependencies and relocations. Changed metadata must not serve stale machine code; interrupted/corrupt entries should fall back safely. Preserve uncached/cold/warm/edited module correctness, run complete Ashui browser apps and CPU-sensitive samples, and measure memory/disk/compile time before choosing a default. Record what can actually be reused and any limitations before promising incremental LLVM builds.","files":null}]}